course: dictionary
lesson: 11213
title: Words of wonder
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words belong to stories, poems and old books more than to everyday talk. Wondrous is one of them. Dictionaries mark it literary, so you will meet it in fairy tales, hymns and old books rather than in a chat about lunch.
explain: Wondrous means amazing and wonderful, so it describes something that causes wonder. Look at the clue: «and all the wondrous world that it led to». The world at the end of the road sounds far off and exciting, so wondrous fits: it makes you want to find out more.
explain: Wondrous comes from wonder, the feeling of being amazed. A good test is to ask, "Does this make me stop and stare?" If it does, wondrous probably fits. Then swap in amazing and check that the sentence still makes sense.
fact: Wondrous means amazing and wonderful, causing wonder.
fact: Wondrous is a literary word: writers use it in stories, poems and hymns.
fact: Wonder is the feeling of being amazed by something.
fact: «Wondrous sights» and «a wondrous story» are typical phrases.
fact: Science can explain a snowflake or a rainbow and it can still be wondrous.
exercise: «and all the wondrous world that it led to» What does wondrous mean here? | Quiet and empty | *Amazing and full of wonder | Dusty and plain | Very close by
exercise: Mia says the snowflake was wondrous to look at. Which word is closest in meaning? | Ordinary | Cold | *Amazing | Heavy
exercise: Tom says wondrous is just a fancy word for big. What is the best check? | Count the letters in the word | Ask someone to guess | *Swap in amazing and see if the sentence still makes sense | Look only at the first letter
test: Which sentence uses wondrous correctly? | The bus was wondrous late again | The rain was wondrous, so we stayed in | *The night sky was wondrous, full of stars that made us gasp | The cake was wondrous, and we ate it all in one go
test: Why can a rainbow still be wondrous even when science explains it? | *Because knowing how it works does not stop it amazing us | Because scientists cannot see colours | Because it is a secret that no one can explain | Because it only lasts a moment
exam: Which word is closest in meaning to wondrous? | Ordinary | Dreary | Tiny | *Marvellous
